---
title: Users, groups & roles
description: Modelling who exists and what ends up in their token.
section: Configuring a tenant
---

GateKeeper's access model is deliberately small: **users**, **groups**, and **roles**. That is all.

> [!IMPORTANT]
> There is no separate "permissions" entity. Roles are *names*, not bundles of fine-grained
> permissions — GateKeeper puts them in the token and **your application decides what they mean**.
> If you are looking for a screen to tick individual permissions on, there isn't one, and that is
> by design.

## The three concepts

- **User** — one person. Has credentials, a profile, and MFA enrolments.
- **Group** — a set of users. Use it for *organizational* structure: "Engineering", "Acme Corp".
- **Role** — a capability name. Use it for *authorization*: "admin", "reporting_viewer".

Groups and roles are assigned independently. A user can be in three groups and hold two roles.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    U["User<br/>ana@example.com"]
    G1["Group: Engineering"]
    G2["Group: Acme Corp"]
    R1["Role: admin"]
    R2["Role: reporting_viewer"]
    T["Access token<br/>groups: [...]<br/>roles: [...]"]

    U --> G1
    U --> G2
    U --> R1
    U --> R2
    G1 --> T
    G2 --> T
    R1 --> T
    R2 --> T
```

Both land in the token, as the `groups` and `roles` claims. Your API reads those claims and
enforces its own rules.

## Roles are re-resolved on every token

This is the part worth internalising.

A user's roles and groups are looked up fresh **every time a token is issued — including on
refresh.** They are not frozen into a long-lived session at login.

The practical consequence is good: revoking a role takes effect at the user's next token refresh
(within the 15-minute access-token lifetime), not whenever they happen to log out. You do not have
to hunt down and kill sessions to remove someone's access.

The flip side: an access token already in flight keeps whatever it was minted with until it
expires. Access tokens are valid for 15 minutes, so that is your worst-case window.

## Creating users

Admins can create users directly (**Users → Create**), which is how you provision accounts when
tenant self-registration is off.

When you set a temporary password, tick **must change password**. The user is then forced through
a password change on first sign-in, and the password you typed is never a long-term credential.

> [!TIP]
> If self-registration is on, users create themselves and confirm their email; you rarely create
> users by hand. Which of the two you're doing is decided by the tenant's
> **[self-registration setting](/docs/first-tenant)**.

## The user detail page

Three tabs:

- **Overview** — profile, status, when they last signed in.
- **Access** — their groups and roles, and where to change them.
- **Privacy** — the GDPR/LGPD surface: export their data, erase them, review which policy versions
  they accepted, and revoke an application's consent. See
  [Data subject requests](/docs/data-subject-requests).

## A worked example

Say you are running a B2B product. A reasonable model:

- One **tenant** for production.
- One **group** per customer company — so you can see who belongs to Acme.
- **Roles** for capability: `owner`, `member`, `reporting_viewer`.

Your API then trusts the `roles` claim for permission checks, and uses the `groups` claim to scope
queries to the right customer's data. GateKeeper never needs to know what "reporting_viewer" means.
